tags: govt_affairs, cabinet
#subtitle: Major changes to the 1931 Constitution.

= Constitutional Reform

Many in the neorevisionist current consider the 1931 Constitution, hurriedly drafted in the Republic's first year, to have baked in some of the instability that has plagued the government ever since. They believe amendments are needed to build a more stable democratic republic - ideas shared by <span style="color: #c00000;">socialists</span> and left-republicans alike.

A constitutional reform requires either a two-thirds supermajority in the Cortes, or a constitutional referendum with support from a majority of the electorate. If we lack a supermajority, we must be assured of strong support from the other parties for the referendum to pass.

@reforms_menu

- @vote_threshold: Reform the electoral law's majority premium.
- @presidential_powers: Curb the President's power to dissolve the Cortes.
- @as_is: Do not pursue constitutional reform.
- @easy_discard
- @cancel_advisor_action

@vote_threshold
subtitle: Reduce the "premio de mayoría" that awards the largest bloc in each district a disproportionate share of its seats.
on-arrival: {!
Q.reform_support = Q.psoe_normalized + Q.izq_rep_normalized;
if (Q.pce_relation > 50 && Q.pce_normalized >= 0.1) {
    Q.reform_support += Q.pce_normalized;
}
!}
view-if: electoral_threshold = 0

The 1931 electoral law's majority premium has produced landslide swings at every election so far - the same law that gave the Republican-Socialist coalition 63% of seats in 1931 could hand the CEDA-Radical bloc an equally lopsided majority next time. Some argue this makes for strong governments; others argue it makes for wild, destabilizing swings that discredit parliamentary government itself.

Smaller and more evenly-matched parties tend to favor tempering the premium. [? if pce_relation > 50 and pce_normalized >= 0.1 : Perhaps surprisingly, the <span style="color: #700000;">**PCE**</span> supports this reform, hoping a fairer system might finally win it seats of its own. ?]

The Caballerista left of our own party is more skeptical, fearing that a more proportional system might weaken a future Popular Front's ability to govern decisively.

- @vote_threshold_2: Call a constitutional referendum to pass the reform!
- @reforms_menu: See other reform options.


@vote_threshold_2
choose-if: reform_support >= pass_threshold
unavailable-subtitle: We do not have enough support for this policy. [? if pro_republic < 65 : We should have an additional margin for the referendum, since we need a majority of voters, and many voters who do not support the Republic will not participate. ?]
on-arrival: constitutional_reform += 1; electoral_threshold = 5; radical_relation -= 15; izq_rep_relation -= 5; left_dissent += 20
achievement: verfassungsreform

We have passed a constitutional amendment tempering the majority premium of the electoral law. Future elections should produce results closer to the parties' actual vote shares - for better or worse.


@presidential_powers
subtitle: The President can no longer dissolve the Cortes at will more than once in a term without cause shown to a special Cortes commission.
on-arrival: {!
Q.reform_support = Q.psoe_normalized + Q.izq_rep_normalized + Q.pce_normalized;
if (Q.radical_relation > 55) {
    Q.reform_support += Q.radical_normalized;
}
!}
view-if: presidential_powers = 0

Article 81 grants the President sweeping power to dissolve the Cortes - a power Alcalá-Zamora has already shown he is willing to use for his own political ends. We can support a reform requiring the President to justify a second dissolution within the same term to a special Cortes commission, closing the door on the kind of arbitrary dissolution that could otherwise let a hostile President simply keep calling elections until he gets the Cortes he wants.

The <span style="color: #700000;">**PCE**</span> and most republicans favor this reform. [? if radical_relation > 55 : The Radical Party, wary of Alcalá-Zamora's unpredictability, is also inclined to support it. ?][? if radical_relation <= 55 : The Radical Party is unconvinced, wary of anything that might weaken the presidency as an institution. ?] The CEDA and the monarchist right oppose any reduction of presidential authority that might come at their own expense should they ever hold the office.

- @presidential_powers_2: Call a constitutional referendum to pass the reform!
- @reforms_menu: See other reform options.

@presidential_powers_2
choose-if: reform_support >= pass_threshold
unavailable-subtitle: We do not have enough support for this policy. [? if pro_republic < 65 : We should have an additional margin for the referendum, since we need a majority of voters, and many voters who do not support the Republic will not participate. ?]
on-arrival: constitutional_reform += 1; presidential_powers = 1
achievement: verfassungsreform

We have successfully passed a constitutional amendment curbing the President's power of dissolution. Whatever happens next, it will not be because Alcalá-Zamora dissolved an inconvenient Cortes on a whim.

@as_is
go-to: root
